{
  "difficulty": "intermediate",
  "score": 6,
  "estimated_minutes": 55,
  "factors": {
    "transaction_background": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "The reviewer must distinguish COPIC’s loaned securities, borrowers’ cash collateral, and COPIC’s Trust shares to understand why redeeming shares and obtaining return of loaned securities raised different exit questions."
    },
    "domain_knowledge": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "Basic securities-lending mechanics and cash-versus-in-kind redemption require explanation, but the defects do not require technical knowledge of structured investment vehicles, portfolio valuation, or Rule 2a-7."
    },
    "defect_spread": {
      "level": "high",
      "note": "Understanding the liability and investment-authority disputes requires reading Declaration Sections 3.1 and 8.1 against SLA Sections 2(f) and 8 and the Confidential Memoranda, while the exit disputes require separate comparisons involving Section 5.2 and SLA Section 4."
    },
    "court_reasoning": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "Lines 77–87 explain the disputed readings directly, but the reviewer must distinguish the court’s construction of Section 8.4 from its alternative, unresolved readings of Section 8.1 and the documents’ respective primacy."
    },
    "issue_distinctness": {
      "level": "high",
      "note": "The Section 8.1 disputes share passages but concern different questions—an exclusive liability list, conflicting negligence thresholds, and the scope-of-authority prerequisite—and must also be separated from the SLA’s allocation of investment losses."
    }
  },
  "hardest_defect": "d11 — The reviewer must understand that Section 8.1’s scope-of-authority prerequisite may independently limit immunity for investments contrary to the SLA’s objectives, rather than merely changing the negligence threshold. That requires separating this reading from the same passage’s alternative explanations concerning conflicting standards and a non-exclusive liability list.",
  "reason": "Assign this to a reviewer comfortable comparing related transaction documents, rather than requiring a financial-products specialist. The main challenge is keeping the Declaration’s immunity provisions, the SLA’s investment duties, and the allocation of investment losses analytically separate. The reviewer should map those provisions and distinguish redemption of Trust shares from return of the securities held by borrowers. The court supplies clear explanations, but several are possible readings sufficient to defeat summary judgment, not final resolutions of contractual meaning."
}
